WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.365
Lesson 6.0.

00:00:02.365 --> 00:00:06.106
The Mastery Starter for Module 6: Protocol Research.

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:07.910
By the end, you'll know the five

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:10.042
words this module runs on, and exactly

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:12.010
how to tell when you've mastered it.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.600
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.215
First, the sixty-second version: the same six-step loop

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:21.831
a professional actually runs, every single time, before

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:24.455
ever trusting a protocol with money.

00:00:24.455 --> 00:00:26.132
Second, the five words you'll

00:00:26.132 --> 00:00:27.830
need, each one drawn out.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.245
Third, what to have ready before you start.

00:00:30.245 --> 00:00:32.879
Fourth, your first safe step: running that

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:35.690
six-step loop on one real protocol today.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:38.205
And finally, the mastery ladder, so you know

00:00:38.205 --> 00:00:40.775
exactly what finishing this module looks like.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.640
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.393
Before trusting any protocol with real money, a professional

00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:51.653
researches it the same exact way, every single time:

00:00:51.653 --> 00:00:55.037
what it actually does, where its money genuinely comes

00:00:55.037 --> 00:00:58.605
from, what it actually depends on, whether it's genuinely

00:00:58.605 --> 00:01:02.358
solvent, what the real evidence actually says, and precisely

00:01:02.358 --> 00:01:03.765
how you'd get out.

00:01:03.765 --> 00:01:06.700
This module teaches you that same repeatable loop.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.931
Here's that loop, laid out in full, the exact

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:14.035
same six steps this entire module builds on.

00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:16.810
Mechanism: what it actually does, and how.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.775
Cash flow: where its money genuinely comes from.

00:01:19.775 --> 00:01:22.962
Dependencies: everything it actually relies on,

00:01:22.962 --> 00:01:25.785
from Module five's own dependency map.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:29.216
Solvency: whether it can genuinely cover what it owes.

00:01:29.216 --> 00:01:32.993
Evidence: what real, verifiable data actually says.

00:01:32.993 --> 00:01:35.015
And exit: precisely how, and how

00:01:35.015 --> 00:01:37.098
fast, you could genuinely get out.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:41.125
Here's all six steps, named individually, since

00:01:41.125 --> 00:01:43.682
each one asks a genuinely different question

00:01:43.682 --> 00:01:46.424
you'll come back to throughout this module.

00:01:46.424 --> 00:01:48.899
Mechanism: what does it actually do.

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:52.179
Cash flow: where does its money genuinely come from.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:55.254
Dependencies: what does it actually rely on.

00:01:55.254 --> 00:01:58.365
Solvency: can it genuinely cover what it owes.

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:02.255
Evidence: what does real, verifiable data actually say.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.951
And exit: how, and how fast,

00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:05.530
could you actually get out.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.867
Worth being direct about why running this exact

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:13.221
same loop, every time, actually matters, rather than

00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:16.685
improvising a fresh process for each new protocol.

00:02:16.685 --> 00:02:20.320
Consistency is what actually lets you compare one protocol

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.709
against another honestly, and it's what stops you from

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.913
skipping the one specific step that happens to feel

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:30.795
unnecessary for whatever protocol currently has your attention.

00:02:32.095 --> 00:02:33.870
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.085
Word one: due diligence.

00:02:37.085 --> 00:02:41.025
Structured research, run before you ever invest, not after.

00:02:41.025 --> 00:02:43.400
Here's the actual sequence it follows.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.717
You define the specific questions worth

00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:48.045
answering, using the six-step loop.

00:02:48.045 --> 00:02:51.510
You gather real, verifiable evidence for each one.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.033
And you reach an actual verdict, with a

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:57.187
size, conditions, and an exit, rather than simply

00:02:57.187 --> 00:02:59.900
a vague overall feeling about the protocol.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.115
Word two: T.V.L., total value locked.

00:03:04.115 --> 00:03:07.650
The total value currently deposited in a protocol.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.465
It's a genuinely useful signal of real, current usage,

00:03:11.465 --> 00:03:14.100
but it's not, on its own, any kind of

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.123
safety guarantee; a protocol can hold enormous T.V.L. and

00:03:18.123 --> 00:03:22.145
still fail, exactly the way this module's earlier lessons

00:03:22.145 --> 00:03:24.850
on infrastructure risk already covered.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.855
Word three: F.D.V., fully diluted valuation.

00:03:29.855 --> 00:03:32.550
Token price, multiplied by the maximum

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.545
supply that could ever genuinely exist.

00:03:35.545 --> 00:03:39.465
It's worth comparing directly against a protocol's own actual,

00:03:39.465 --> 00:03:42.577
current market cap, since a large gap between the

00:03:42.577 --> 00:03:45.190
two tells you a great deal about how much

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.675
future dilution current holders are actually exposed to.

00:03:49.975 --> 00:03:53.702
Here's why checking F.D.V. against current market cap

00:03:53.702 --> 00:03:57.730
actually matters, using a purely illustrative example.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.640
A token might show a current market cap of

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.903
one hundred million dollars, genuinely tradeable today, but an

00:04:04.903 --> 00:04:08.422
F.D.V. of one billion, ten times larger, once every

00:04:08.422 --> 00:04:11.970
token that could ever exist is actually counted.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.500
That gap is real future dilution, worth

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.650
knowing before you buy, not after.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.040
Quick check.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.235
What does F.D.V. actually measure?

00:04:25.655 --> 00:04:28.651
The answer: token price, multiplied by the

00:04:28.651 --> 00:04:31.925
maximum supply that could ever genuinely exist.

00:04:33.225 --> 00:04:35.040
Word four: governance.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.464
How a protocol's own token holders actually vote on

00:04:38.464 --> 00:04:43.045
real changes: upgrades, parameters, treasury spending, all of it.

00:04:43.045 --> 00:04:46.951
Worth checking specifically how concentrated that voting power

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:50.422
actually is, since governance controlled by a genuinely

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:53.894
small handful of large holders behaves very differently

00:04:53.894 --> 00:04:56.560
from governance spread broadly across many.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.695
Word five: thesis.

00:04:59.695 --> 00:05:02.686
What genuinely has to be true, specifically, for a

00:05:02.686 --> 00:05:05.860
position to actually work out the way you expect.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.111
Writing it down explicitly is what lets you actually

00:05:09.111 --> 00:05:12.731
notice, later, the exact moment that thesis breaks, rather

00:05:12.731 --> 00:05:15.675
than simply continuing to hold out of habit, or

00:05:15.675 --> 00:05:18.865
hope, once the real conditions have already changed.

00:05:20.165 --> 00:05:21.255
Quick check.

00:05:21.255 --> 00:05:22.819
What's the actual purpose of

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:24.760
writing down your thesis explicitly?

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.825
The answer: it lets you genuinely notice the

00:05:31.825 --> 00:05:34.704
moment it breaks, rather than continuing to hold

00:05:34.704 --> 00:05:36.350
purely out of habit or hope.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:38.695
Before you start.

00:05:39.995 --> 00:05:42.014
Here's what this module assumes you're

00:05:42.014 --> 00:05:44.540
already comfortable with, before diving in.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.046
Everything from Module zero through two:

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:49.790
wallets, networks, swaps, and liquidity.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.480
Modules three and four: lending, leverage,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.095
and where yield genuinely comes from.

00:05:55.095 --> 00:05:57.642
And Module five, in full: the complete

00:05:57.642 --> 00:06:01.038
dependency map, since dependencies are exactly step

00:06:01.038 --> 00:06:03.715
three of this module's own six-step loop.

00:06:05.015 --> 00:06:06.290
Your first safe step.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.389
Here's exactly what to do, before this module

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:13.245
goes any deeper into each individual step.

00:06:13.245 --> 00:06:15.521
Pick any protocol you've genuinely heard of,

00:06:15.521 --> 00:06:17.995
one you're at least somewhat familiar with.

00:06:17.995 --> 00:06:21.180
Write exactly one sentence for each of the six

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:26.360
steps: mechanism, cash flow, dependencies, solvency, evidence, and exit.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.891
And if you genuinely don't know the answer for

00:06:28.891 --> 00:06:32.015
one, write don't know yet, honestly, rather than guessing.

00:06:33.315 --> 00:06:35.963
Worth being direct about why don't know yet

00:06:35.963 --> 00:06:39.272
is a genuinely acceptable, even valuable, answer here,

00:06:39.272 --> 00:06:42.220
rather than something to avoid writing down.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.220
It tells you exactly where your own

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.130
research actually has to go next, specifically.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.132
A confident guess, dressed up as a real answer,

00:06:50.132 --> 00:06:53.510
is far more dangerous than an honestly incomplete one.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:55.905
The mastery ladder.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:58.910
Here's the first two rungs on

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.200
this module's ladder, side by side.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.015
At the beginner level, you can complete the full

00:07:04.015 --> 00:07:07.245
six-step loop, with real sources backing each step.

00:07:07.245 --> 00:07:11.010
At the practitioner level, you go further: genuinely reading

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:15.022
tokenomics and governance directly, and writing an actual thesis

00:07:15.022 --> 00:07:18.355
that includes its own specific invalidation condition.

00:07:19.655 --> 00:07:22.931
Here's what practitioner-level tokenomics and governance

00:07:22.931 --> 00:07:25.530
reading actually looks like, concretely.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.511
Check how concentrated token holdings genuinely

00:07:28.511 --> 00:07:30.737
are, among the largest wallets.

00:07:30.737 --> 00:07:33.143
Check exactly how much voting power

00:07:33.143 --> 00:07:36.717
that same concentration actually translates into.

00:07:36.717 --> 00:07:39.636
And write your thesis with a specific, real

00:07:39.636 --> 00:07:43.682
invalidation condition attached, not simply a general, vague

00:07:43.682 --> 00:07:46.137
sense of optimism about the protocol.

00:07:47.436 --> 00:07:49.753
And here's the top rung, master level,

00:07:49.753 --> 00:07:52.726
the one this module ultimately builds toward.

00:07:52.726 --> 00:07:56.003
At that level, you can genuinely spot failure patterns

00:07:56.003 --> 00:07:59.459
early, often before they're obvious to everyone else, and

00:07:59.459 --> 00:08:02.915
write verdicts other people can actually act on directly,

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.822
not just your own private notes.

00:08:06.122 --> 00:08:08.808
Here's how you'll actually know you've mastered

00:08:08.808 --> 00:08:11.122
this entire module, in one sentence.

00:08:11.122 --> 00:08:14.394
Your due-diligence file ends in a real verdict,

00:08:14.394 --> 00:08:17.802
specifically with a size, real conditions, and an

00:08:17.802 --> 00:08:20.870
actual exit, every single time, not simply a

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.937
vague, unresolved impression of the protocol.

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:27.282
What a real verdict actually looks like.

00:08:28.582 --> 00:08:31.567
Here's what a real verdict genuinely looks like,

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:34.547
in practice, not as an abstract requirement.

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:37.202
A size: a specific, capped dollar amount,

00:08:37.202 --> 00:08:40.157
decided in advance, not an open-ended one.

00:08:40.157 --> 00:08:43.217
Conditions: the specific facts that currently make

00:08:43.217 --> 00:08:46.517
this position acceptable, written down explicitly.

00:08:46.517 --> 00:08:48.778
And an exit: the exact condition that

00:08:48.778 --> 00:08:51.397
would actually make you leave, decided now,

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:54.372
before you're emotionally attached to the outcome.

00:08:55.672 --> 00:08:56.917
Let's recap.

00:08:56.917 --> 00:09:02.161
The six-step loop: mechanism, cash flow, dependencies, solvency, evidence,

00:09:02.161 --> 00:09:05.327
exit, run the same way every single time.

00:09:05.327 --> 00:09:07.512
The five words: due diligence,

00:09:07.512 --> 00:09:10.562
T.V.L., F.D.V., governance, and thesis.

00:09:10.562 --> 00:09:13.358
Your first safe step is running that loop, in

00:09:13.358 --> 00:09:16.697
one sentence per step, on one real protocol today.

00:09:16.697 --> 00:09:19.580
And you'll know you've mastered this module when

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.874
your file ends in a real verdict, with

00:09:21.874 --> 00:09:23.757
a size, conditions, and an exit.

00:09:25.057 --> 00:09:27.731
Pick one real protocol today, and write one

00:09:27.731 --> 00:09:30.584
honest sentence for each of the six steps.

00:09:30.584 --> 00:09:34.131
Next up, Lesson 6.1: protocol due diligence.
